From: WinBoat
Subject: [PATCH] venus, wsi/win32: fast Vulkan presents on Windows

Presenting a Vulkan frame took 15.6 ms however small the window (63 fps),
and the application waited for all of it.

- os_time_sleep on Windows slept with Sleep(), which lasts at least one
  tick of the system timer (15.6 ms by default). Venus waits by polling with
  short sleeps (vn_relax starts at 160 us), so every wait for the GPU took a
  whole tick. Sleep on a high-resolution waitable timer instead (Windows 10
  1803 and later; older Windows keep Sleep()).
- Win32 WSI's software path draws straight from the mapped image with
  StretchDIBits instead of copying it into a DIB section first.
- The software path presents on a thread of its own: it waits for the
  image's rendering and draws it while the application renders the next
  frame. FIFO and MAILBOX keep to the compositor's pace (DwmFlush), and
  IMMEDIATE and MAILBOX are offered too (MAILBOX drops a waiting frame when a
  newer one comes). Swapchains get at least 2 images (3 for MAILBOX).
  wsi_swapchain::present_waits_for_fence tells the common code not to wait
  for the fence itself.
- Venus no longer waits for the queue to go idle before a present on
  Windows: the software path waits for the image's fence before it reads it.

1200x781 window in the spike: 63 fps -> 1093 fps (IMMEDIATE), FIFO at the
display's 60 Hz, MAILBOX 1373 frames/s rendered; swapchains recreated with
frames still queued work in all three modes.
---
